{"type":"message","id":"b71f11b4","parentId":"9428ca1a","timestamp":"2026-06-24T08:38:19.522Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01_OhchEfIPvTpaAsKb9Tcx1552","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# GitHub Issue #86 Analysis\n\n> **Source:** https://github.com/vhallac/semblr/issues/86\n> **Title:** Adapt sakana.ai's TRINITY into multi-model routing\n> **Author:** vhallac (project owner)\n> **Created:** 2026-06-23\n> **Updated:** 2026-06-24\n> **State:** open\n> **Comments:** 2\n> **Labels:** none\n> **Assignees / Milestone:** none\n\n## 1. Issue Summary\n\nThe issue proposes adapting Sakana AI's **TRINITY** mechanism — a small classifier network that\nchooses which of three backend LLMs should handle a given turn — for Semblr. Instead of a tiny\nexternal classifier, the LLM itself is asked to self-report its current **generation phase** via a\nnew Semblr tool; Semblr then switches to a different model between agent cycles when the phase\nwarrants it.\n\nThe \"Background\" section in the issue body describes TRINITY as a small network that maintains a\nhidden state, selects one of three backend models, and feeds each one the previous turn's tokens,\ntool results, and a pre-cooked prompt. TRINITY is **not** an LLM.\n\nThe \"Idea\" section observes that Semblr already injects prompts to make the LLM summarize / wrap up\nwhen context grows too long. The proposal is to extend that same injection mechanism with a\ndedicated `semblr_report_phase` tool whose parameters capture both an `upcoming generation phase`\nenum and a free-form \"note to future self\". Semblr then uses these to route the next turn to a\ndifferent model.\n\nThe \"Problems\" section already flags the major risks: no prior art for LLM self-assessment, weaker\nmodels may not use the tool reliably, pi may block mid-stream model switches, and every model\nswitch breaks KV-cache (incurring uncached-token cost).\n\n## 2. Requirements Extracted From Issue + Comments\n\n### 2.1 From the original issue body\n\n1. **A new tool** the LLM can call to report its upcoming generation phase (analogous to how\n   Semblr's existing `semblr_checkpoint` is used to wrap up oversized contexts).\n2. The tool must expose **at least one parameter** — the upcoming generation phase — with a\n   suggested set of values: `exploring`, `executing`, `stuck`, `verifying`, `done`.\n3. A **second optional parameter**: a \"note to future self\" that is forwarded to the next model\n   if a switch happens.\n4. **Model switching happens at agent-cycle boundaries** (between rounds), not mid-response.\n5. Semblr must be able to **store phase + note per round** and pass the note into the next\n   round's context as continuity.\n6. The mechanism should be **tunable per phase** — some phases should route to a faster/cheaper\n   worker, some should escalate to a stronger model, and some should keep the current model.\n\n### 2.2 From comment #1 (2026-06-24, MVP specification)\n\nThis is the **authoritative spec for the MVP** — clearly delineated in/out scope.\n\n**In scope for MVP:**\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| One new tool | `semblr_report_phase` |\n| Six phases (enum) | `exploring`, `planning`, `executing`, `stuck`, `verifying`, `reporting` |\n| Two tool parameters | `phase` (required, literal union); `note` (optional, **EXPERIMENTAL** string) |\n| Routing at `agent_end` only | Switches occur between agent cycles |\n| Config toggle | `semblr.routing.enabled` (default: `false`); env var: `SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED=true` |\n| Output-stream announcement | e.g. `Semblr: switching to anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514 for execution phase` |\n| Visible error on switch failure | If `pi.setModel()` returns `false`, surface a styled error in the output stream |\n| Hardcoded switch limiter | Maximum **3** model switches per agent cycle (not configurable) |\n| Phase-note injection | When a switch happens, the new model sees the note in context as `[PHASE TRANSITION NOTE]` |\n| Hardcoded phase→model map | (see §2.4) |\n\n**Out of scope for MVP (deferred):**\n\n- No presets (e.g. `fast-sonnet`, `dual-model`, `escalation-only`).\n- No user-facing `phaseModels` config mapping — map is hardcoded for MVP.\n- No config knobs: `maxSwitchesPerCycle`, `maxConsecutiveStuck`, `minTurnsPerPhase`,\n  `announceSwitches`, `escalationModel`, `logLevel`.\n- No `/semblr:routing` slash command — enable/disable via config file or env var only.\n- No TUI integration beyond output-stream messages (no status bar widget, no phase indicators).\n- Note parameter marked `EXPERIMENTAL` — explicitly called out as a flag to revisit.\n\n**Tool signature (TypeBox, from comment #1):**\n\n```typescript\nType.Object({\n  phase: Type.Union([\n    Type.Literal(\"exploring\"),\n    Type.Literal(\"planning\"),\n    Type.Literal(\"executing\"),\n    Type.Literal(\"stuck\"),\n    Type.Literal(\"verifying\"),\n    Type.Literal(\"reporting\"),\n  ], {\n    description: \"Your current generation phase. 'exploring': pulling in external data by reading, searching, etc. 'planning': formulating a plan of response. 'executing': implementing the plan. 'stuck': underspecified, insufficient data — fishing by extensive exploration or guessing. 'verifying': execution done, validating output and created files. 'reporting': done, about to report the final output.\"\n  }),\n  note: Type.Optional(Type.String({\n    description: \"EXPERIMENTAL: A note to the next model that handles this task. Share context, decisions made, open questions, or suggestions for how to proceed. Keep it concise.\"\n  })),\n})\n```\n\n**Tool metadata:**\n\n- `name`: `semblr_report_phase`\n- `label`: `Report Phase`\n- `promptSnippet`: `Report your current generation phase for multi-model routing`\n\n**Switch logic (comment #1, step 6 of \"What Happens When\"):** at `agent_end`, Semblr checks\nall four of:\n\n1. Is routing enabled?\n2. Was a phase reported this round?\n3. Is the target model different from the current model?\n4. Has the switch limit (3 per cycle) been exceeded?\n\nOnly if all four pass does `pi.setModel(targetModel)` fire. If `pi.setModel()` fails, an error\nis surfaced in the output stream (styled like LLM errors) and Semblr stays on the current model.\n\nComment #1 step 10 also specifies a **fallback ceiling**: `If >5 switches in a single agent\ncycle → routing is suppressed for the remainder of that cycle and a warning is emitted. We\nwill allow for explore -> plan -> execute -> verify -> report.` This is a slight tension with\nthe \"3 per cycle\" limiter (the cap is 3, the absolute suppression is 5). The author may have\nintended a hard ceiling at 3 + a soft grace limit at 5, or there may be a small inconsistency\nworth raising in the implementation PR. **Open question — see §7.**\n\n### 2.3 From comment #2 (2026-06-24, Ollama-Cloud model map)\n\nThis comment **replaces** the Anthropic/Sonnet examples in the MVP spec with an Ollama-Cloud\nphase→model table, and adds detailed capability requirements per phase. Treat this as the\n**effective** hardcoded map for the MVP (replacing the Anthropic example from comment #1).\n\n**Effective hardcoded `MVP_PHASE_MODEL_MAP` (from comment #2, code block at the bottom):**\n\n```typescript\nconst MVP_PHASE_MODEL_MAP: Record<string, string | null> = {\n  exploring: null,                  // no switch — user's current model\n  planning:  \"deepseek-v4-flash:cloud\",\n  executing: \"glm-5.2:cloud\",\n  stuck:     \"kimi-k2.6:cloud\",\n  verifying: \"minimax-m3:cloud\",\n  reporting: \"gemma4:12b:cloud\",\n};\n```\n\n`null` → stay on the current model. All other entries trigger `pi.setModel()` if the active\nmodel differs. Note the spec uses **Ollama-Cloud naming convention** (`<model>:cloud`).\n\n**Phase capability definitions (comment #2):**\n\n| Phase | Capability demand | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `exploring` | Crucial — large context, thoroughness, low hallucination, good tool discipline | \"No switch\": user controls exploration capability budget |\n| `planning` | Medium — distillation, structured thinking, 256K+ ctx, latency-sensitive | A good place to optimize for speed |\n| `executing` | High — strong coding, precise tool use, long-horizon reliability, 1M context | `glm-5.2:cloud` confirmed |\n| `stuck` | Critical — creative problem-solving, deep reasoning, debugging intuition, autonomy, swarm | `kimi-k2.6:cloud` \"user's leaning\"; alt `deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` |\n| `verifying` | Medium — methodical, tool-orchestration, attention to detail | `minimax-m3:cloud` |\n| `reporting` | Low — summarization, formatting, very fast | `gemma4:12b:cloud` (or smaller) |\n\n**Cost/latency tier annotations** (comment #2, last table):\n\n| Phase | Model | Cost tier | Latency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| exploring | deepseek-v4-pro (rec.) | extra high | slowest |\n| planning | deepseek-v4-flash | medium | fast |\n| executing | glm-5.2 | high | medium |\n| stuck | kimi-k2.6 | high | medium-slow |\n| verifying | minimax-m3 | high | medium |\n| reporting | gemma4:12b | low | very fast |\n\n**Alternative swaps flagged by the author** (worth knowing for future config, but NOT for MVP):\n\n- `kimi-k2.6` → `deepseek-v4-pro` for stuck (1M ctx vs 256K, higher cost).\n- `kimi-k2.6` → `kimi-k2.7-code` for stuck (30% fewer thinking tokens, less proven).\n- `minimax-m3` → `deepseek-v4-flash` for verifying (cheaper, less agentic).\n- `gemma4:12b` → `deepseek-v4-flash` for reporting (1M ctx, more expensive).\n- `glm-5.2` → `kimi-k2.7-code` for executing (slightly better real-world coding, no 1M ctx).\n\n### 2.4 Combined acceptance criteria for the MVP\n\nSynthesizing both comments, the MVP must:\n\n1. **Register a new tool** `semblr_report_phase` in `session_start` (consistent with the other\n   Semblr tools) with the exact TypeBox schema and metadata above.\n2. **Accept and store** `(phase, note)` per round somewhere the `agent_end` handler can read it.\n3. **Be opt-in**: default off; enabled by `semblr.routing.enabled: true` in settings.json OR\n   `SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED=true` env var.\n4. **Switch at `agent_end`** using the hardcoded map in §2.3 — `null` means no switch.\n5. **Enforce the 3-switch-per-cycle limit** (with the >5 absolute suppression as a\n   question-flagged edge case — see §7).\n6. **Inject the `note` as `[PHASE TRANSITION NOTE]`** in the next context assembly when a\n   switch actually occurs.\n7. **Emit a visible output-stream line** like\n   `Semblr: switching to <model> for <phase> phase` on success.\n8. **Surface a styled error** in the output stream if `pi.setModel()` returns `false`, and\n   stay on the current model.\n9. **Not** add a slash command, TUI widget, or user-facing config knobs in MVP.\n\n## 3. Feature Branch Recommendation\n\n**`feature/86-multi-model-routing`**\n\nRationale: matches the project convention (issue # → \"feature/N-short-description\"), captures\nthe core idea (\"multi-model routing\") without being so long that the name is unwieldy. Shorter\nalternates considered and rejected:\n\n- `feature/86-trinity-routing` — too specific to a third-party paper; the spec deliberately\n  diverges from TRINITY.\n- `feature/86-llm-self-routing` — accurate but the spec itself uses \"multi-model routing\" as\n  the canonical name.\n- `feature/86-routing-mvp` — leaves ambiguity about whether the MVP-vs-full distinction lives\n  in branch names; the spec says MVP is the only thing being built first.\n\n## 4. Current Codebase Context\n\n### 4.1 Architecture overview\n\nSemblr is a **single pi extension** (`src/semblr.ts`, ~1480 lines) backed by ~50 helper modules\nin `lib/`. The extension factory registers a handful of event hooks (`session_start`,\n`agent_start`, `message_end`, `agent_end`, `context`, `session_before_compact`) and a few\nslash commands. It is loaded as a pi package (not auto-loaded) and currently exposes 7\ntools: `search_interactions`, `get_round_details`, `get_tool_details`, `semblr_checkpoint`,\n`mini_mem__add`, `mini_mem__get`, `mini_mem__update`, `mini_mem__delete`,\n`mini_mem__get_and_delete`.\n\n### 4.2 Key files relevant to issue #86\n\n| File | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| `src/semblr.ts` | All extension glue. Where `pi.setModel()` would be called from `agent_end`. Where the new tool must be registered. |\n| `lib/state.ts` | Defines `SessionState` (lifetime: session) and `RoundState` (lifetime: one user prompt → full response). The new routing state (reported phase, note, switch counter) belongs on `RoundState`; the global switch-counter and total counter may belong on `SessionState` (see §5). |\n| `lib/semblr-config.ts` | The single source of truth for `SemblrConfig`. New config key (`routing.enabled`) and env var (`SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED`) need to be added here. The existing pattern uses a `ConfigKey` mapped to an `ENV_KEYS` entry plus a `resolveBoolean` (would be new) or extension of `resolveString`/`resolveNumber` to handle a boolean. |\n| `lib/semblr-config.test.ts` | Will need a new test verifying `routing.enabled: false` default + env + project/global override matrix. |\n| `lib/round-data.ts` | `RoundData` may want a new optional field `reportedPhase?: { phase: string; note: string \\| null }` so the phase+note persist into the round JSON for observability. Not strictly required by the spec but would aid debugging. |\n| `lib/state.test.ts` | Will need new test cases covering the new state fields and the reset semantics. |\n| `README.md`, `VISION.md` | Both currently describe Semblr as context-assembly only. Will need updates once the feature ships. |\n| `index.ts` | Tiny re-export — no change needed. |\n\n### 4.3 Existing patterns the new feature should follow\n\nThese are the conventions I extracted from the existing extension — the new code should\nmirror them.\n\n1. **Tools are registered inside `session_start`**, not at factory level. The comment in\n   `semblr.ts` (line 1305) explains: \"registerTool is called inside session_start because\n   factory-level registration doesn't reliably make tools visible to the LLM.\" The new\n   `semblr_report_phase` tool must follow the same pattern.\n\n2. **State lifetime mirrors pi's event model.** `createSession()` is called at\n   `session_start`; `createRound()` is called at `agent_start`. Anything that should survive\n   across many user prompts in one session lives on `SessionState`; anything that lives for a\n   single user-prompt → response cycle lives on `RoundState`.\n\n3. **Config resolution pattern** (lib/semblr-config.ts):\n   - Add a `ConfigKey` literal.\n   - Add a `DEFAULTS` entry.\n   - Add an `ENV_KEYS` entry.\n   - Add a `resolveBoolean` helper (analogous to `resolveString` / `resolveNumber`) OR\n     handle the boolean case via a new `resolveBoolean` function.\n   - Add the key to the returned object from `loadSemblrConfig`.\n   - Add tests in `lib/semblr-config.test.ts` (defaults, env override, project override,\n     invalid values, etc.).\n\n4. **Output-stream messaging pattern**: existing code uses\n   `ctx.ui.setStatus(\"semblr\", \"…\")` for status-bar text. The spec for the new feature\n   demands a \"visible in the output stream\" line — `ctx.ui.notify(level, message)` is the\n   right API for that (it surfaces to the user as a toast / inline block). Look at how\n   `semblr:import-claude` uses `ctx.ui.notify` for precedent (see lines 360–368 of\n   `semblr.ts`).\n\n5. **Tools return shape**:\n   ```typescript\n   return {\n     content: [{ type: \"text\", text: \"...\" }],\n     details: {},\n   };\n   ```\n   The new tool should match this.\n\n6. **Hardcoded constants are placed at module top** with `const NAME = …;` style. The\n   `MVP_PHASE_MODEL_MAP` from comment #2 belongs at the top of the file (or in a new\n   `lib/routing.ts` module) as a frozen, exported constant.\n\n7. **The existing `semblr_checkpoint` tool** is the closest analogue to\n   `semblr_report_phase` — both are \"internal-style\" tools that the LLM is steered toward via\n   the system context. The existing pattern registers `semblr_checkpoint` with a\n   description that says \"INTERNAL: DO NOT call unless instructed\" (line 1390-ish). The new\n   tool's description should follow the *opposite* pattern — it *should* be called by the\n   LLM as part of normal generation, not as an emergency escape valve.\n\n8. **The extension's contract with pi** uses `pi.registerTool`, `pi.registerCommand`, and\n   `pi.on(event, handler)`. There is no \"declarative config\" — everything is imperative\n   inside the factory.\n\n### 4.4 What does NOT exist yet (gap analysis)\n\n| Capability needed | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `pi.setModel` invocation | Never used by Semblr before. |\n| `pi.setModel` model-by-id lookup | Must construct a `Model<any>` from the string id. Semblr would need to walk `ctx.modelRegistry.getAll()` and match by `id` (or by `provider/id` if namespacing). |\n| Per-cycle switch counter | New state field on `RoundState` (or `SessionState`, see §5). |\n| Per-session total switch counter | New state field if the absolute >5 suppression is real. |\n| Note injection in `context` hook | New section; follows the existing pattern of `FollowUpMsg` / `CheckpointMsg` injection (see `resolveCompoundInjections` in `semblr.ts` ~line 510). |\n| Settings option for routing | New key in `SemblrConfig` and new env var. |\n| `MVP_PHASE_MODEL_MAP` | New constant. |\n| Output-stream switch announcement | New code path in `agent_end`. |\n\n## 5. Implementation Approach Outline\n\nBelow is a concrete sketch — not code, but a plan of where the new pieces land. Follows the\nproject's \"code-and-test-together\" methodology in AGENTS.md.\n\n### Step 1 — `lib/semblr-config.ts`: add `routing.enabled`\n\n- Add `routingEnabled: boolean` to the `SemblrConfig` interface.\n- Add a `ConfigKey` literal `routingEnabled`.\n- Add a `DEFAULTS.routingEnabled = false` entry.\n- Add an `ENV_KEYS.routingEnabled = \"SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED\"` entry.\n- Extend the `SemblrConfigEnv` interface with the new env var.\n- Add a `resolveBoolean` helper (parses `\"true\"/\"1\"` as `true`, `\"false\"/\"0\"` as `false`,\n  warns on invalid). Reuse the existing `resolveValue` to honour env → project → global\n  precedence.\n- Wire it into the returned config object.\n- Add tests in `lib/semblr-config.test.ts` for: default `false`, env override `true`,\n  project settings override, invalid value warning + fallback. Update the \"returns\n  defaults\" test to include `routingEnabled: false`.\n\n### Step 2 — `lib/routing.ts` (new): pure helpers\n\nCreate `lib/routing.ts` exporting:\n\n- `REPORTED_PHASES` — the `[\"exploring\",\"planning\",\"executing\",\"stuck\",\"verifying\",\"reporting\"]`\n  tuple. Type `as const` so TypeScript can derive the union.\n- `MVP_PHASE_MODEL_MAP: Readonly<Record<Phase, string | null>>` — exact value from\n  comment #2. Note the `null` for `exploring`.\n- `MAX_SWITCHES_PER_CYCLE = 3` and `ABSOLUTE_SWITCH_LIMIT = 5` constants.\n- `buildSwitchAnnouncement(targetModel: string, phase: Phase): string` — pure formatter.\n- `buildPhaseTransitionNote(phase: Phase, note: string | null): string | null` — pure\n  formatter for the `[PHASE TRANSITION NOTE]` context block.\n- `isReportedPhase(value: unknown): value is Phase` — type guard for tool input.\n\nAll of these are pure → easy to test without pi infrastructure.\n\nCreate `lib/routing.test.ts` covering:\n\n- The phase union and map match comment #2 exactly (data integrity test).\n- `buildSwitchAnnouncement` produces the exact format in the spec.\n- `buildPhaseTransitionNote` returns `null` when note is empty, formats correctly otherwise.\n- `isReportedPhase` accepts only the six literals, rejects `null`, `undefined`, and any\n  other string.\n\n### Step 3 — `lib/state.ts`: add routing fields\n\n- Add to `RoundState`:\n  - `reportedPhase: Phase | null` — the LLM's most recent report this round.\n  - `phaseNote: string | null` — the LLM's note, if provided.\n  - `phaseSwitchesThisCycle: number` — running co